  skos:prefLabel "Reading—General" .

ceds:NI001517163646
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "English/Literature (freshmen and sophomores) courses are designed for freshmen and/or sophomores and typically introduce them to two or more genres of literature (novel, short story, poetry, and so on). Exploration of each genre's literary elements; determination of theme and intent; and examination of vocabulary and semantics are often included in the course content. Writing assignments are required as an additional method to improve understanding and comprehension." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163646"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Literature (freshmen and sophomores)" ;
  skos:definition "English/Literature (freshmen and sophomores) courses are designed for freshmen and/or sophomores and typically introduce them to two or more genres of literature (novel, short story, poetry, and so on). Exploration of each genre's literary elements; determination of theme and intent; and examination of vocabulary and semantics are often included in the course content. Writing assignments are required as an additional method to improve understanding and comprehension." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01051" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Literature (freshmen and sophomores)" .

ceds:NI001517163647
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """English/Literature (juniors and seniors) courses are designed for juniors
			and/or seniors and emphasize comprehension, discernment, and critical-thinking skills in
			the reading of texts and literature. These courses introduce and explore more advanced
			literary techniques (irony, satire, humor, connotation, tone, rhythm, symbolism, and so
			on) through two or more literary genres, with the aim of creating sophisticated readers.
			Writing assignments are required as an additional method to develop and improve
			critical-thinking and analytic skills.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163647"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Literature (juniors and seniors)" ;
  skos:definition """English/Literature (juniors and seniors) courses are designed for juniors
			and/or seniors and emphasize comprehension, discernment, and critical-thinking skills in
			the reading of texts and literature. These courses introduce and explore more advanced
			literary techniques (irony, satire, humor, connotation, tone, rhythm, symbolism, and so
			on) through two or more literary genres, with the aim of creating sophisticated readers.
			Writing assignments are required as an additional method to develop and improve
			critical-thinking and analytic skills.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01052" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Literature (juniors and seniors)" .

ceds:NI001517163648
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Literature courses offer the opportunity for students to study and reflect upon the themes presented in the body of literature being presented. Students improve their critical-thinking skills as they determine the underlying assumptions and values within the reading selection and as they understand how the work reflects society's problems and culture. Oral discussion is an integral part of literature courses, and written compositions are often required. Literature courses may survey representative works, reflect a particular genre or a specific theme, or survey works of a particular time or people." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163648"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Literature" ;
  skos:definition "Literature courses offer the opportunity for students to study and reflect upon the themes presented in the body of literature being presented. Students improve their critical-thinking skills as they determine the underlying assumptions and values within the reading selection and as they understand how the work reflects society's problems and culture. Oral discussion is an integral part of literature courses, and written compositions are often required. Literature courses may survey representative works, reflect a particular genre or a specific theme, or survey works of a particular time or people." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01053" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Literature" .

