			topography, the patterns and processes of climate and weather, and natural resources.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "03007" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Physical Geography" .

ceds:NI001517163329
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Earth and Space Science courses introduce students to the study of the earth
			from a local and global perspective. In these courses, students typically learn about
			time zones, latitude and longitude, atmosphere, weather, climate, matter, and energy
			transfer. Advanced topics often include the study of the use of remote sensing, computer
			visualization, and computer modeling to enable earth scientists to understand earth as a
			complex and changing planet.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163329"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Earth and Space Science" ;
  skos:definition """Earth and Space Science courses introduce students to the study of the
			earth from a local and global perspective. In these courses, students typically learn
			about time zones, latitude and longitude, atmosphere, weather, climate, matter, and
			energy transfer. Advanced topics often include the study of the use of remote sensing,
			computer visualization, and computer modeling to enable earth scientists to understand
			earth as a complex and changing planet.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "03008" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Earth and Space Science" .

