ceds:NI001517163456
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """World Area Studies courses examine the history, politics, economics,
			society, and/or culture of one or more regions of the world, such as Africa, Latin
			America, the former Soviet Union, Far East Asia, and the Middle East. These courses may
			focus primarily on the history of a particular region or may take an interdisciplinary
			approach to the contemporary issues affecting the region. Furthermore, these courses may
			emphasize one particular country (other than the United States), rather than emphasizing
			a region or continent.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163456"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "World Area Studies" ;
  skos:definition """World Area Studies courses examine the history, politics, economics,
			society, and/or culture of one or more regions of the world, such as Africa, Latin
			America, the former Soviet Union, Far East Asia, and the Middle East. These courses may
			focus primarily on the history of a particular region or may take an interdisciplinary
			approach to the contemporary issues affecting the region. Furthermore, these courses may
			emphasize one particular country (other than the United States), rather than emphasizing
			a region or continent.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "04061" ;
  skos:prefLabel "World Area Studies" .

ceds:NI001517163457
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "World People Studies courses allow students to study various types of subgroups that have something in common such as religion, gender, or culture. Similar in style to World Area Studies, but focusing on a group of people rather than on a specific region, these courses examine a subgroup's history, politics, economics, and/or culture." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163457"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "World People Studies" ;
  skos:definition "World People Studies courses allow students to study various types of subgroups that have something in common such as religion, gender, or culture. Similar in style to World Area Studies, but focusing on a group of people rather than on a specific region, these courses examine a subgroup's history, politics, economics, and/or culture." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "04062" ;
  skos:prefLabel "World People Studies" .

ceds:NI001517163458
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Western Civilization courses apply an interdisciplinary approach to the
			study of western cultural traditions, frequently using a chronological framework. Course
			content typically includes a survey of the major developments in and contributors to art
			and architecture, literature, religion and philosophy, and culture. These courses may
			also cover intellectual and political movements.""" ;
