ceds:NI001517164104
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Health Science courses integrate chemistry, microbiology, chemical
			reactions, disease processes, growth and development, and genetics with anatomy and
			physiology of the body systems. Typically, these courses reinforce science, mathematics,
			communications, health, and social studies principles and relate them to health care.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164104"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Health Science" ;
  skos:definition """Health Science courses integrate chemistry, microbiology, chemical
			reactions, disease processes, growth and development, and genetics with anatomy and
			physiology of the body systems. Typically, these courses reinforce science, mathematics,
			communications, health, and social studies principles and relate them to health care.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "14251" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Health Science" .

ceds:NI001517164105
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Biotechnology courses involve the study of the bioprocesses of organisms,
			cells, and/or their components and enable students to use this knowledge to produce or
			refine products, procedures, and techniques. Course topics typically include laboratory
			measurement, monitoring, and calculation; growth and reproduction; chemistry and biology
			of living systems; quantitative problem-solving; data acquisition and display; and
			ethics. Advanced topics may include elements of biochemistry, genetics, and protein
			purification techniques.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164105"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Biotechnology" ;
  skos:definition """Biotechnology courses involve the study of the bioprocesses of organisms,
			cells, and/or their components and enable students to use this knowledge to produce or
			refine products, procedures, and techniques. Course topics typically include laboratory
			measurement, monitoring, and calculation; growth and reproduction; chemistry and biology
			of living systems; quantitative problem-solving; data acquisition and display; and
			ethics. Advanced topics may include elements of biochemistry, genetics, and protein
			purification techniques.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "14252" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Biotechnology" .

ceds:NI001517164106
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Pharmacology courses involve a study of how living animals can be changed by
			chemical substances, especially by the actions of drugs and other substances used to
			treat disease. Basic concepts of physiology, pathology, biochemistry, and bacteriology
			are typically brought into play as students examine the effects of drugs and their
			mechanisms of action.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164106"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Pharmacology" ;
  skos:definition """Pharmacology courses involve a study of how living animals can be changed
