ceds:NI001517163158
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "English/Language Arts I (9th grade) courses build upon students' prior knowledge of grammar, vocabulary, word usage, and the mechanics of writing and usually include the four aspects of language use: reading, writing, speaking, and listening. Typically, these courses introduce and define various genres of literature, with writing exercises often linked to reading selections." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163158"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Language Arts I (9th grade)" ;
  skos:definition "English/Language Arts I (9th grade) courses build upon students' prior knowledge of grammar, vocabulary, word usage, and the mechanics of writing and usually include the four aspects of language use: reading, writing, speaking, and listening. Typically, these courses introduce and define various genres of literature, with writing exercises often linked to reading selections." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01001" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Language Arts I (9th grade)" .

ceds:NI001517163159
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "English/Language Arts II (10th grade) courses usually offer a balanced focus on composition and literature. Typically, students learn about the alternate aims and audiences of written compositions by writing persuasive, critical, and creative multi-paragraph essays and compositions. Through the study of various genres of literature, students can improve their reading rate and comprehension and develop the skills to determine the author's intent and theme and to recognize the techniques used by the author to deliver his or her message." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163159"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Language Arts II (10th grade)" ;
  skos:definition "English/Language Arts II (10th grade) courses usually offer a balanced focus on composition and literature. Typically, students learn about the alternate aims and audiences of written compositions by writing persuasive, critical, and creative multi-paragraph essays and compositions. Through the study of various genres of literature, students can improve their reading rate and comprehension and develop the skills to determine the author's intent and theme and to recognize the techniques used by the author to deliver his or her message." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01002" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Language Arts II (10th grade)" .

ceds:NI001517163160
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "English/Language Arts III (11th grade) courses continue to develop students' writing skills, emphasizing clear, logical writing patterns, word choice, and usage, as students write essays and begin to learn the techniques of writing research papers. Students continue to read works of literature, which often form the backbone of the writing assignments. Literary conventions and stylistic devices may receive greater emphasis than in previous courses." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163160"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Language Arts III (11th grade)" ;
  skos:definition "English/Language Arts III (11th grade) courses continue to develop students' writing skills, emphasizing clear, logical writing patterns, word choice, and usage, as students write essays and begin to learn the techniques of writing research papers. Students continue to read works of literature, which often form the backbone of the writing assignments. Literary conventions and stylistic devices may receive greater emphasis than in previous courses." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01003" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Language Arts III (11th grade)" .

ceds:NI001517163161
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """English/Language Arts IV (12th grade) courses blend composition and
			literature into a cohesive whole as students write critical and comparative analyses of
			selected literature, continuing to develop their language arts skills. Typically,
			students primarily write multi-paragraph essays, but they may also write one or more
			major research papers.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163161"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "English/Language Arts IV (12th grade)" ;
  skos:definition """English/Language Arts IV (12th grade) courses blend composition and
			literature into a cohesive whole as students write critical and comparative analyses of
			selected literature, continuing to develop their language arts skills. Typically,
			students primarily write multi-paragraph essays, but they may also write one or more
			major research papers.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01004" ;
  skos:prefLabel "English/Language Arts IV (12th grade)" .

ceds:NI001517163162
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Following the College Board's suggested curriculum designed to parallel college-level English courses, AP English Language and Composition courses expose students to prose written in a variety of periods, disciplines, and rhetorical contexts. These courses emphasize the interaction of authorial purpose, intended audience, and the subject at hand, and through them, students learn to develop stylistic flexibility as they write compositions covering a variety of subjects that are intended for various purposes." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163162"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "AP English Language and Composition" ;
  skos:definition "Following the College Board's suggested curriculum designed to parallel college-level English courses, AP English Language and Composition courses expose students to prose written in a variety of periods, disciplines, and rhetorical contexts. These courses emphasize the interaction of authorial purpose, intended audience, and the subject at hand, and through them, students learn to develop stylistic flexibility as they write compositions covering a variety of subjects that are intended for various purposes." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01005" ;
  skos:prefLabel "AP English Language and Composition" .

ceds:NI001517163163
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Following the College Board's suggested curriculum designed to parallel college-level English courses, AP English Literature and Composition courses enable students to develop critical standards for evaluating literature. Students study the language, character, action, and theme in works of recognized literary merit; enrich their understanding of connotation, metaphor, irony, syntax, and tone; and write compositions of their own (including literary analysis, exposition, argument, narrative, and creative writing)." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163163"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "AP English Literature and Composition" ;
  skos:definition "Following the College Board's suggested curriculum designed to parallel college-level English courses, AP English Literature and Composition courses enable students to develop critical standards for evaluating literature. Students study the language, character, action, and theme in works of recognized literary merit; enrich their understanding of connotation, metaphor, irony, syntax, and tone; and write compositions of their own (including literary analysis, exposition, argument, narrative, and creative writing)." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01006" ;
  skos:prefLabel "AP English Literature and Composition" .

