ceds:NI001517163808
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Introduction to Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) courses
			introduce students to the purposes and objectives of the Reserve Officer Training Corps
			program, which seeks to educate high school students in citizenship, promote community
			service, and instill responsibility. As part of that introduction, course topics
			typically include a brief history of the military branches in the United States and the
			basics of military drill, ceremony, and rank structure.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163808"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Introduction to Jr. ROTC" ;
  skos:definition """Introduction to Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) courses
			introduce students to the purposes and objectives of the Reserve Officer Training Corps
			program, which seeks to educate high school students in citizenship, promote community
			service, and instill responsibility. As part of that introduction, course topics
			typically include a brief history of the military branches in the United States and the
			basics of military drill, ceremony, and rank structure.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09001" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Introduction to Jr. ROTC" .

ceds:NI001517163809
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Although individual course sequences may vary, the primary objectives of
			Military Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) courses are to provide students
			with instruction in the history, organization, role, objectives, and achievements of a
			particular branch of the U.S. Armed Forces; help them develop personal fitness, strong
			character, and leadership qualities; and expose them to the career opportunities
			provided by the U.S. Armed Services. These courses typically cover such topics as
			military customs, courtesies, rank, drill, and ceremonies and also emphasize citizenship
			and scholarship. The course content typically includes subjects related to the
			particular branch being studied (such as map-reading, nautical skills, aerospace
			technology, or communication technologies), as well as more general subjects
			(international law, national defense, celestial navigation, and geopolitical strategy).""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163809"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Military Jr. ROTC—unspecified branch" ;
  skos:definition """Although individual course sequences may vary, the primary objectives of
			Military Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) courses are to provide students
			with instruction in the history, organization, role, objectives, and achievements of a
			particular branch of the U.S. Armed Forces; help them develop personal fitness, strong
			character, and leadership qualities; and expose them to the career opportunities
			provided by the U.S. Armed Services. These courses typically cover such topics as
			military customs, courtesies, rank, drill, and ceremonies and also emphasize citizenship
			and scholarship. The course content typically includes subjects related to the
			particular branch being studied (such as map-reading, nautical skills, aerospace
			technology, or communication technologies), as well as more general subjects
			(international law, national defense, celestial navigation, and geopolitical strategy).""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09002" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Military Jr. ROTC—unspecified branch" .

ceds:NI001517163810
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) Drill courses provide students with an
			additional opportunity to improve their skills in military precision. These courses
			emphasize marching style and formations, firearm manipulation, body coordination and
			mechanics, and performing as a member of an orchestrated team. Class members typically
			participate in ceremonies and competitions.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163810"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "ROTC Drill" ;
  skos:definition """Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) Drill courses provide students with
			an additional opportunity to improve their skills in military precision. These courses
			emphasize marching style and formations, firearm manipulation, body coordination and
			mechanics, and performing as a member of an orchestrated team. Class members typically
			participate in ceremonies and competitions.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09003" ;
  skos:prefLabel "ROTC Drill" .

ceds:NI001517163811
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Military Leadership courses focus solely on increasing students' leadership skills, particularly as they relate to military operations, customs, and hierarchies. These courses are typically a regular part of the ROTC programs described below (typically the final course within a program series); this Military Leadership course code and title should be used when those descriptions do not apply. The principles and skills taught in these courses include supervision, motivation, evaluation, and setting an example, and their application typically include military drill and inspections, athletic events, and other school activities." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163811"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Military Leadership" ;
  skos:definition "Military Leadership courses focus solely on increasing students' leadership skills, particularly as they relate to military operations, customs, and hierarchies. These courses are typically a regular part of the ROTC programs described below (typically the final course within a program series); this Military Leadership course code and title should be used when those descriptions do not apply. The principles and skills taught in these courses include supervision, motivation, evaluation, and setting an example, and their application typically include military drill and inspections, athletic events, and other school activities." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09004" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Military Leadership" .

ceds:NI001517163812
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) I courses include
			instruction in the organization and functions of the U.S. Army, leadership skills, and
			life skills education. The content of these courses cover (but is not limited to) the
			history and evolution of the Army, including its structure, operations, customs and
			courtesies; maps and navigation; first aid, personal hygiene, and field sanitation; and
			substance abuse prevention. These courses also introduce students to principles of
			leadership and citizenship.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163812"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Army Junior ROTC I" ;
  skos:definition """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) I courses include
			instruction in the organization and functions of the U.S. Army, leadership skills, and
			life skills education. The content of these courses cover (but is not limited to) the
			history and evolution of the Army, including its structure, operations, customs and
			courtesies; maps and navigation; first aid, personal hygiene, and field sanitation; and
			substance abuse prevention. These courses also introduce students to principles of
			leadership and citizenship.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09051" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Army Junior ROTC I" .

ceds:NI001517163813
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) II courses build upon the
			content of Army Junior ROTC I and include (but are not limited to) ongoing instruction
			in leadership principles and citizenship; drill and ceremonies; organizational
			structure; command and staff relationships, functions, and responsibilities; significant
			military campaigns and leaders; map-reading and orienteering; weapon safety and
			marksmanship; and survival training.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163813"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Army Junior ROTC II" ;
  skos:definition """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) II courses build upon the
			content of Army Junior ROTC I and include (but are not limited to) ongoing instruction
			in leadership principles and citizenship; drill and ceremonies; organizational
			structure; command and staff relationships, functions, and responsibilities; significant
			military campaigns and leaders; map-reading and orienteering; weapon safety and
			marksmanship; and survival training.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09052" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Army Junior ROTC II" .

ceds:NI001517163814
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) III courses build upon prior Army Junior ROTC courses, giving more emphasis to leadership development. These courses serve to strengthen students' leadership skills (including planning, problem-solving, motivation, and performance appraisal) and management skills (with regard to time, personnel, and other resources) through allowing them to assume leadership duties. Students study topics introduced in earlier years—such as military history, map-reading and orienteering, marksmanship, and drill and ceremonies—at a more advanced level and are also provided with military service opportunities." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163814"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Army Junior ROTC III" ;
  skos:definition "Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) III courses build upon prior Army Junior ROTC courses, giving more emphasis to leadership development. These courses serve to strengthen students' leadership skills (including planning, problem-solving, motivation, and performance appraisal) and management skills (with regard to time, personnel, and other resources) through allowing them to assume leadership duties. Students study topics introduced in earlier years—such as military history, map-reading and orienteering, marksmanship, and drill and ceremonies—at a more advanced level and are also provided with military service opportunities." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09053" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Army Junior ROTC III" .

ceds:NI001517163815
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) IV courses focus on
			practical leadership by assigning students to command and staff positions in which they
			present instruction to lower Army Junior ROTC classes and continue to study and review
			staff functions and actions, staff-commander relationships, and leadership principles.
			Topics introduced in earlier years may be studied at more advanced levels.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163815"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Army Junior ROTC IV" ;
  skos:definition """Army Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) IV courses focus on
			practical leadership by assigning students to command and staff positions in which they
			present instruction to lower Army Junior ROTC classes and continue to study and review
			staff functions and actions, staff-commander relationships, and leadership principles.
			Topics introduced in earlier years may be studied at more advanced levels.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09054" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Army Junior ROTC IV" .

ceds:NI001517163816
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Naval Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) I courses emphasize
			citizenship and leadership development, as well as maritime heritage, sea power, and
			Naval operations and customs. These courses include (but are not limited to) an
			introduction to the Naval Junior ROTC program, U.S. Navy mission and organization,
			maritime geography, naval history, basic seamanship, oceanography, and health education.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163816"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Naval Junior ROTC I" ;
  skos:definition """Naval Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) I courses emphasize
			citizenship and leadership development, as well as maritime heritage, sea power, and
			Naval operations and customs. These courses include (but are not limited to) an
			introduction to the Naval Junior ROTC program, U.S. Navy mission and organization,
			maritime geography, naval history, basic seamanship, oceanography, and health education.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "09101" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Naval Junior ROTC I" .

ceds:NI001517163817
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Naval Junior Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) II courses build upon the
			content of Naval Junior ROTC I. These courses include (but are not limited to)
			leadership principles and discipline, citizenship, naval opportunities and career
			planning, naval ships and weaponry, seamanship, meteorology and weather, and survival
			training. Students continue to learn teamwork, naval history, and military principles.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163817"^^xsd:token ;
