ceds:NI001353117358
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001353, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Those assets that the school district intends to hold or continue to use
			over a long period of time. Specifically, capital assets include land, improvements to
			land, easements, buildings and building improvements, vehicles, machinery, equipment,
			works of art and historical treasures, infrastructure, and all other tangible or
			intangible assets that are used in operations and that have initial useful lives that
			extend beyond a single reporting period. This account is used only in proprietary funds,
			fiduciary funds, and the government-wide financial statements.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001353117358"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Capital Assets" ;
  skos:definition """Those assets that the school district intends to hold or continue to use
			over a long period of time. Specifically, capital assets include land, improvements to
			land, easements, buildings and building improvements, vehicles, machinery, equipment,
			works of art and historical treasures, infrastructure, and all other tangible or
			intangible assets that are used in operations and that have initial useful lives that
			extend beyond a single reporting period. This account is used only in proprietary funds,
			fiduciary funds, and the government-wide financial statements.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001353 ;
  skos:notation "200" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Capital Assets" .

ceds:NI001353117364
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001353, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Tangible property of a more or less permanent nature, other than land,
			buildings, or improvements thereto, that is useful in carrying on operations. Examples
			are machinery, tools, trucks, cars, buses, computers, purchased software, furniture, and
			furnishings. Appendix E provides criteria to distinguish whether a purchase is a supply
			or a piece of machinery or equipment.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001353117364"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Machinery and Equipment" ;
  skos:definition """Tangible property of a more or less permanent nature, other than land,
			buildings, or improvements thereto, that is useful in carrying on operations. Examples
			are machinery, tools, trucks, cars, buses, computers, purchased software, furniture, and
			furnishings. Appendix E provides criteria to distinguish whether a purchase is a supply
			or a piece of machinery or equipment.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001353 ;
  skos:notation "241" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Machinery and Equipment" .

ceds:NI001353117359
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001353, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """A capital asset account that reflects the acquisition value of land owned by
			a school district. If land is purchased, this account includes the purchase price and
			costs such as legal fees, filling and excavation costs, and other associated improvement
			costs incurred to put the land in condition for its intended use. If land is acquired by
			gift, the account reflects its fair value at the time of acquisition. Permanent
			improvements to land, such as grading and fill, should also be accounted for in this
			account. Land and land improvements are considered nonexhaustible assets owing to their
			significantly long expected useful life. Nonexhaustible assets are not to be
			depreciated. Therefore, assets classified by asset code 211 should result in no
			depreciation expense.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001353117359"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Land and Land Improvements" ;
  skos:definition """A capital asset account that reflects the acquisition value of land owned
			by a school district. If land is purchased, this account includes the purchase price and
			costs such as legal fees, filling and excavation costs, and other associated improvement
			costs incurred to put the land in condition for its intended use. If land is acquired by
			gift, the account reflects its fair value at the time of acquisition. Permanent
			improvements to land, such as grading and fill, should also be accounted for in this
			account. Land and land improvements are considered nonexhaustible assets owing to their
			significantly long expected useful life. Nonexhaustible assets are not to be
			depreciated. Therefore, assets classified by asset code 211 should result in no
			depreciation expense.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001353 ;
  skos:notation "211" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Land and Land Improvements" .

ceds:NI001353117360
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001353, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """A capital asset account that reflects the value of nonpermanent improvements
			to building sites, other than buildings, that add value to land. Examples of such
			improvements are fences, retaining walls, sidewalks, pavements, gutters, tunnels, and
			bridges. If the improvements are purchased or constructed, this account contains the
			purchase or contract price. If improvements are obtained by gift, it reflects the fair
			value at the time of acquisition. Site improvements are improvements that have a limited
			useful life. Because these improvements decrease in their value/usefulness over time, it
			is appropriate to depreciate these assets. Therefore, all capitalized site improvements
			should be depreciated over their expected useful life.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001353117360"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Site Improvements" ;
  skos:definition """A capital asset account that reflects the value of nonpermanent
			improvements to building sites, other than buildings, that add value to land. Examples
			of such improvements are fences, retaining walls, sidewalks, pavements, gutters,
			tunnels, and bridges. If the improvements are purchased or constructed, this account
			contains the purchase or contract price. If improvements are obtained by gift, it
			reflects the fair value at the time of acquisition. Site improvements are improvements
			that have a limited useful life. Because these improvements decrease in their
			value/usefulness over time, it is appropriate to depreciate these assets. Therefore, all
			capitalized site improvements should be depreciated over their expected useful life.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001353 ;
  skos:notation "221" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Site Improvements" .

ceds:NI001353117361
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001353, skos:Concept ;
