			presentation or oral communication skills. Specific content depends upon state standards
			for grade 3.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01031" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Language Arts (grade 3)" .

ceds:NI001517163184
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Language Arts (grade 4) courses include the four aspects of language use:
			reading, writing, speaking, and listening. These courses may emphasize independent
			reading and writing in a variety of assignments and require students to respond to
			different material in different ways. Grammar and usage of the English language may also
			be emphasized. Specific content depends upon state standards for grade 4.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163184"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Language Arts (grade 4)" ;
  skos:definition """Language Arts (grade 4) courses include the four aspects of language use:
			reading, writing, speaking, and listening. These courses may emphasize independent
			reading and writing in a variety of assignments and require students to respond to
			different material in different ways. Grammar and usage of the English language may also
			be emphasized. Specific content depends upon state standards for grade 4.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01032" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Language Arts (grade 4)" .

ceds:NI001517163185
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Language Arts (grade 5) courses include the four aspects of language use: reading, writing, speaking, and listening. These courses may extend students' skills in composition, writing for particular purposes or audiences, distinguishing meaning and literary functions in texts, and fluency in reading. Specific content depends upon state standards for grade 5." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163185"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Language Arts (grade 5)" ;
  skos:definition "Language Arts (grade 5) courses include the four aspects of language use: reading, writing, speaking, and listening. These courses may extend students' skills in composition, writing for particular purposes or audiences, distinguishing meaning and literary functions in texts, and fluency in reading. Specific content depends upon state standards for grade 5." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01033" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Language Arts (grade 5)" .

ceds:NI001517163186
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Biblical Literature courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on the books of the Bible. Students may compare techniques, styles, and themes of the various books; examine the Bible's influence on secular literature; and may study historical events of Biblical times. Oral discussion is an integral part of these courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163186"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Biblical Literature" ;
  skos:definition "Biblical Literature courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on the books of the Bible. Students may compare techniques, styles, and themes of the various books; examine the Bible's influence on secular literature; and may study historical events of Biblical times. Oral discussion is an integral part of these courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01059" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Biblical Literature" .

ceds:NI001517163187
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "These courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on a particular author and his or her work. Students determine the underlying assumptions and values within the selected works; compare techniques, styles, and themes of the author; and reflect upon the time period in which the author lived. Oral discussion is an integral part of literature courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163187"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Literature of an Author" ;
  skos:definition "These courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on a particular author and his or her work. Students determine the underlying assumptions and values within the selected works; compare techniques, styles, and themes of the author; and reflect upon the time period in which the author lived. Oral discussion is an integral part of literature courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01060" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Literature of an Author" .

ceds:NI001517163188
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "These courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on one or several genres, such as poetry, essay, biography, short story, drama, film, and so on. Students determine the underlying assumptions and values within the selected works and also examine the structure, techniques, and intentions of the genre being studied. Oral discussion is an integral part of these genre-oriented courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163188"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Literature of a Genre" ;
  skos:definition "These courses have the same aim as general literature courses (to improve students' language arts and critical-thinking skills), focusing on one or several genres, such as poetry, essay, biography, short story, drama, film, and so on. Students determine the underlying assumptions and values within the selected works and also examine the structure, techniques, and intentions of the genre being studied. Oral discussion is an integral part of these genre-oriented courses, and written compositions are often required." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01061" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Literature of a Genre" .

ceds:NI001517163189
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Language Arts (grade 6) courses build upon students' prior knowledge of grammar, vocabulary, word usage, and the mechanics of writing, and include the four aspects of language use: reading, writing, speaking, and listening. These courses may emphasize the use of language for different effects, in different contexts, and for different purposes. Specific content depends upon state standards for grade 6." ;
