ceds:NI001517163239
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Public Speaking courses enable students, through practice, to develop
			communication skills that can be used in a variety of speaking situations (such as small
			and large group discussions, delivery of lectures or speeches in front of audiences, and
			so on). Course topics may include (but are not limited to) research and organization,
			writing for verbal delivery, stylistic choices, visual and presentation skills, analysis
			and critique, and development of self-confidence.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163239"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Public Speaking" ;
  skos:definition """Public Speaking courses enable students, through practice, to develop
			communication skills that can be used in a variety of speaking situations (such as small
			and large group discussions, delivery of lectures or speeches in front of audiences, and
			so on). Course topics may include (but are not limited to) research and organization,
			writing for verbal delivery, stylistic choices, visual and presentation skills, analysis
			and critique, and development of self-confidence.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01151" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Public Speaking" .

ceds:NI001517163240
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Forensic Speech—Inclusive courses offer students the opportunity to learn how to use oral skills effectively in formal and informal situations. Students learn such skills as logic and reasoning, the organization of thought and supporting materials, and effective presentation of one's voice and body. Often linked to an extracurricular program, these courses introduce students to numerous public speaking situations, and they learn the methods, aims, and styles of a variety of events (e.g., formal debate, Lincoln-Douglas debate, expository speaking, radio broadcast, oral interpretation, and dramatic interpretation). Participation in competition is encouraged, but not always required." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163240"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Forensic Speech—Inclusive" ;
  skos:definition "Forensic Speech—Inclusive courses offer students the opportunity to learn how to use oral skills effectively in formal and informal situations. Students learn such skills as logic and reasoning, the organization of thought and supporting materials, and effective presentation of one's voice and body. Often linked to an extracurricular program, these courses introduce students to numerous public speaking situations, and they learn the methods, aims, and styles of a variety of events (e.g., formal debate, Lincoln-Douglas debate, expository speaking, radio broadcast, oral interpretation, and dramatic interpretation). Participation in competition is encouraged, but not always required." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01152" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Forensic Speech—Inclusive" .

ceds:NI001517163241
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Forensic Speech—Debate courses offer students the opportunity to learn how to use oral skills in formal and informal situations. In these courses, students are able to develop such skills as logic and reasoning, research and analysis, organization of thought and supporting materials, argumentative style and skill, and effective presentation of one's voice and body. Often linked to an extracurricular program, these courses introduce students to the methods, aims, and styles used in various kinds of debates (formal debate or Lincoln-Douglas). Participation in competition is encouraged, but not always required." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517163241"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Forensic Speech—Debate" ;
  skos:definition "Forensic Speech—Debate courses offer students the opportunity to learn how to use oral skills in formal and informal situations. In these courses, students are able to develop such skills as logic and reasoning, research and analysis, organization of thought and supporting materials, argumentative style and skill, and effective presentation of one's voice and body. Often linked to an extracurricular program, these courses introduce students to the methods, aims, and styles used in various kinds of debates (formal debate or Lincoln-Douglas). Participation in competition is encouraged, but not always required." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "01153" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Forensic Speech—Debate" .

ceds:NI001517163247
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
