  skos:prefLabel "Particular Topics in Environmental Service Systems" .

ceds:NI001517164243
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Formerly Plant Production/Science, Plant Systems/Science courses provide
			knowledge about the propagation of plants for food and fiber. These courses may cover
			such topics as soil science, irrigation, pest and weed control, food and fiber
			processing, and farm operations. These courses may also cover the knowledge and skills
			needed to produce all types of crops or may emphasize a particular area of the
			agricultural industry.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164243"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Plant Systems/Science" ;
  skos:definition """Formerly Plant Production/Science, Plant Systems/Science courses provide
			knowledge about the propagation of plants for food and fiber. These courses may cover
			such topics as soil science, irrigation, pest and weed control, food and fiber
			processing, and farm operations. These courses may also cover the knowledge and skills
			needed to produce all types of crops or may emphasize a particular area of the
			agricultural industry.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "18051" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Plant Systems/Science" .

ceds:NI001517164244
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Formerly known as General Horticulture, Horticultural Science courses expose
			students to the art and science of growing plants, shrubs, trees, flowers, fruits, and
			vegetables. In doing so, they cover a wide variety of topics, including principles of
			plant science, greenhouse and nursery operations, soils and growing media mixtures,
			fruit and vegetable production, turf/golf course management, interior and exterior
			plantscaping, irrigation systems, weed and pest control, and floral design.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164244"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Horticultural Science" ;
  skos:definition """Formerly known as General Horticulture, Horticultural Science courses
			expose students to the art and science of growing plants, shrubs, trees, flowers,
			fruits, and vegetables. In doing so, they cover a wide variety of topics, including
			principles of plant science, greenhouse and nursery operations, soils and growing media
			mixtures, fruit and vegetable production, turf/golf course management, interior and
			exterior plantscaping, irrigation systems, weed and pest control, and floral design.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001517 ;
  skos:notation "18052" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Horticultural Science" .

ceds:NI001517164245
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001517, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Ornamental Horticulture courses provide information regarding the care and
			propagation of plants, flowers, trees, and shrubs, but place a special emphasis on those
			plants that are used for decorative and aesthetic purposes. Because of this particular
			emphasis, Ornamental Horticulture courses usually concentrate on nurseries and
			greenhouses and on the floristry industry.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001517164245"^^xsd:token ;
