ceds:NI001874129092
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Very small pieces of solid or liquid matter such as particles of soot, dust,
			fumes, mists or aerosols. The physical characteristics of particles, and how they
			combine with other particles, are part of the feedback mechanisms of the atmosphere.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129092"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Particulate Matter" ;
  skos:definition """Very small pieces of solid or liquid matter such as particles of soot,
			dust, fumes, mists or aerosols. The physical characteristics of particles, and how they
			combine with other particles, are part of the feedback mechanisms of the atmosphere.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "13668" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Particulate Matter" .

ceds:NI001874129093
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """A pesticide is any substance or mixture of substances intended for
			preventing, destroying, repelling, or mitigating any pest. The health effects of
			pesticides depend on the type of pesticide. Some, such as the organophosphates and
			carbamates, affect the nervous system. Others may irritate the skin or eyes. Some
			pesticides may be carcinogens. Others may affect the hormone or endocrine system in the
			body.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129093"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Pesticides" ;
  skos:definition """A pesticide is any substance or mixture of substances intended for
			preventing, destroying, repelling, or mitigating any pest. The health effects of
			pesticides depend on the type of pesticide. Some, such as the organophosphates and
			carbamates, affect the nervous system. Others may irritate the skin or eyes. Some
			pesticides may be carcinogens. Others may affect the hormone or endocrine system in the
			body.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "02903" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Pesticides" .

ceds:NI001874129094
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "PCBs belong to a broad family of man-made organic chemicals known as chlorinated hydrocarbons. PCBs were domestically manufactured from 1929 until their manufacture was banned in 1979. They have a range of toxicity and vary in consistency from thin, light-colored liquids to yellow or black waxy solids. Due to their non-flammability, chemical stability, high boiling point, and electrical insulating properties, PCBs were used in hundreds of industrial and commercial applications including electrical, heat transfer, and hydraulic equipment. Caulk containing PCBs was used in some buildings, including schools, in the 1950s through the 1970s. Although no longer commercially produced in the United States, PCBs may be present in products and materials produced before the ban. PCBs have been demonstrated to cause cancer, as well as a variety of other adverse health effects on the immune system, reproductive system, nervous system, and endocrine system." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129094"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs)" ;
  skos:definition "PCBs belong to a broad family of man-made organic chemicals known as chlorinated hydrocarbons. PCBs were domestically manufactured from 1929 until their manufacture was banned in 1979. They have a range of toxicity and vary in consistency from thin, light-colored liquids to yellow or black waxy solids. Due to their non-flammability, chemical stability, high boiling point, and electrical insulating properties, PCBs were used in hundreds of industrial and commercial applications including electrical, heat transfer, and hydraulic equipment. Caulk containing PCBs was used in some buildings, including schools, in the 1950s through the 1970s. Although no longer commercially produced in the United States, PCBs may be present in products and materials produced before the ban. PCBs have been demonstrated to cause cancer, as well as a variety of other adverse health effects on the immune system, reproductive system, nervous system, and endocrine system." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "13669" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs)" .

ceds:NI001874129095
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """A propellant is a chemical used to create thrust. The material is usually
			expelled by gas pressure. The pressure may be from a compressed gas, or a gas produced
			by a chemical reaction. It may be a gas, liquid, plasma, or, before the chemical
			reaction, a solid.""" ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129095"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Propellants" ;
  skos:definition """A propellant is a chemical used to create thrust. The material is usually
			expelled by gas pressure. The pressure may be from a compressed gas, or a gas produced
			by a chemical reaction. It may be a gas, liquid, plasma, or, before the chemical
			reaction, a solid.""" ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "13670" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Propellants" .

ceds:NI001874129096
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "Radon comes from the natural, radioactive breakdown of uranium in soil, rock and water and gets into the air. Radon gas decays into radioactive particles that can get trapped in the lungs through breathing. As they break down further, these particles release small bursts of energy. This can damage lung tissue and lead to lung cancer. The most common method used to reduce radon is a vent pipe system and fan, which pulls radon from beneath the building and vents it to the outside." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129096"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Radon" ;
  skos:definition "Radon comes from the natural, radioactive breakdown of uranium in soil, rock and water and gets into the air. Radon gas decays into radioactive particles that can get trapped in the lungs through breathing. As they break down further, these particles release small bursts of energy. This can damage lung tissue and lead to lung cancer. The most common method used to reduce radon is a vent pipe system and fan, which pulls radon from beneath the building and vents it to the outside." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "02902" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Radon" .

ceds:NI001874129097
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description "A refrigerant is a compound used in a heat cycle that reversibly undergoes a phase change from a gas to a liquid. Traditionally, fluorocarbons, especially chlorofluorocarbons were used as refrigerants, but they are being phased out because of their ozone depletion effects. Other refrigerants are ammonia, sulfur dioxide, carbon dioxide, and non-halogenated hydrocarbons such as methane. It is illegal to release refrigerants into the atmosphere (intentional or accidental) because they can cause severe damage to the ozone layer. When CFCs are removed they should be recycled to clean out any contaminants and return it to a usable condition. Refrigerants should never be mixed together. Some CFCs must be managed as hazardous waste even if recycled, and special precautions are required for their transport, depending on the legislation of the country's government." ;
  dc11:identifier "NI001874129097"^^xsd:token ;
  rdfs:label "Refrigerants" ;
  skos:definition "A refrigerant is a compound used in a heat cycle that reversibly undergoes a phase change from a gas to a liquid. Traditionally, fluorocarbons, especially chlorofluorocarbons were used as refrigerants, but they are being phased out because of their ozone depletion effects. Other refrigerants are ammonia, sulfur dioxide, carbon dioxide, and non-halogenated hydrocarbons such as methane. It is illegal to release refrigerants into the atmosphere (intentional or accidental) because they can cause severe damage to the ozone layer. When CFCs are removed they should be recycled to clean out any contaminants and return it to a usable condition. Refrigerants should never be mixed together. Some CFCs must be managed as hazardous waste even if recycled, and special precautions are required for their transport, depending on the legislation of the country's government." ;
  skos:inScheme ceds:C001874 ;
  skos:notation "13671" ;
  skos:prefLabel "Refrigerants" .

ceds:NI001874129098
  a owl:NamedIndividual, ceds:C001874, skos:Concept ;
  dc11:creator "Common Education Data Standards" ;
  dc11:description """Waste not regulated as "hazardous" may contain toxic metals such as mercury
			and lead, etc.""" ;
